This solution involves traversing the linked list twice. The first traversal is used to count the number of nodes in the linked list. The second traversal is used to rearrange the nodes in the linked list based on their values.

In the first traversal, we initialize two pointers, `head` and `curr`, to the head of the linked list. We then iterate through the linked list using the `curr` pointer. For each node, we check if it is even or odd by using the modulo operator (`%`). If the node is even, we add it to the `even` list. If the node is odd, we add it to the `odd` list. We keep track of the tail of each list to efficiently add new nodes.

In the second traversal, we iterate through the linked list again using the `curr` pointer. For each node, we check if it is even or odd. If it is even, we add it to the `even` list. If it is odd, we add it to the `odd` list. We keep track of the tail of each list to efficiently add new nodes.

Finally, we return the head of the modified linked list, which is the head of the `even` list.

